{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The 'shares mechanism' relation asserts a shared computational mechanism (incremental recomputation over a changing attack graph) and binds it with a refutation counterexample. The critic school demands a causal mechanism: SRC_001 states that both 07c3398912d4 and SRC_008 'treat the grounded fixpoint as an edit-sensitive computation,' but it provides no causal account of why or how the isolated node performs this computation — it merely redescribes the shared behavior (cache key determines affected subgraph). No causal mechanism (e.g., a shared algorithmic procedure or inherited data structure that causes the edit-sensitive relabelling) is identified; the content is purely semantic alignment. Because the problem requires a substantive relation and the stance requires a causal mechanism, the absence of any causal explanation makes the proposal fail the problem as conditioned.","target":"57b1ddec406402b8435c3dc348e6d9767ec95b96f329b86e7914ec3ff05352ef"},{"attack":false,"case":"The 'integrates' relation identifies a substantive integration: SRC_002 specifies that 07c3398912d4's post-pass operates on provenance chains carried by committed carriage edges (inherited from SRC_005) over the closure structure (from SRC_003), making the diagnostic a function over a provenance-labelled attack graph. This is a concrete mechanism: the consumption of provenance chains causes the post-pass to depend on the labelled, rather than raw, edge representation. The refutation condition is well-formed and operationally testable. Under the causal-mechanism stance, the post-pass consuming provenance chains on committed edges is the causal mechanism by which the integration occurs; the target therefore satisfies the problem.","target":"9ebecd627d750f7a60c694b9c337119dcc43a0334d6795b6d310c6339aa6b974"}]}